{
  "id": "KP24Z",
  "version": "2.0",
  "title": "Non-surgical wrist fracture treatment",
  "description": "Non-surgical wrist fracture treatment is an outpatient procedure where your doctor treats your broken wrist bones without surgery using a cast or brace. Your doctor will take X-rays to determine which wrist bones are broken and assess the fracture pattern. If the fracture is stable and properly aligned, surgery is usually not necessary. You'll typically wear a cast or brace to immobilize your wrist for 4-8 weeks. You'll need to keep your wrist elevated and use ice to manage swelling and pain. You should avoid heavy gripping and strenuous hand activities during healing. Pain medication helps manage discomfort. Periodic X-rays monitor healing progress. Physical therapy helps restore your wrist strength and motion once the cast is removed. Full healing typically takes 8-12 weeks. Some wrist fractures may need the bones repositioned before casting to ensure proper alignment. The cost of this procedure may vary depending on whether your doctor needs to manually reposition (manipulate) the bone fragments into proper alignment, or whether the fracture can heal in its current position without manipulation.",
